// Original beginner lessons: identity, account access and protecting information.
const h=v=>({t:'h',v}),p=v=>({t:'p',v}),key=v=>({t:'key',v}),tip=v=>({t:'tip',v});
const term=(v,note)=>({t:'new',v,note}),check=(q,a,why)=>({q,a,why}),ref=(title,url)=>({title,url});
module.exports={
 '4.1':{
  objective:'Separate account identity from permission, choose unique passwords or passphrases, explain password-manager trade-offs, and draft an account plan without recording any real secrets.',
  sections:[
   h('1. A login is the start of an access decision'),
   p('Week3 introduced accounts on a device. Now Maya signs into a fictional online learning service. The service must establish which account is making the request, then decide whether that account may read a lesson, edit a private note or administer another user. These are separate questions. Knowing a valid password does not automatically grant every action.'),
   term('Identity, authentication and authorisation','An identity is the account represented in a system. Authentication checks control of an accepted credential for that identity. Authorisation decides which action that identity may perform on a resource. A username often identifies an account but is not a secret credential.'),
   p('Worked example: Maya can open her own progress record but cannot edit Ravi’s record. Both users successfully authenticate. Their permissions differ by resource. A visible Edit button is only an interface; the service must enforce the permission when it receives the request. Hiding the button alone would not establish access control.'),
   h('2. What makes a password useful?'),
   term('Password and passphrase','A password is a secret used to authenticate. A passphrase is a password expressed as a sequence of words or a longer phrase. Length, unpredictability and uniqueness matter; a familiar quote can be long but predictable.'),
   term('Password reuse and credential stuffing','Reuse means using one password for multiple accounts. Credential stuffing means trying already obtained username/password pairs at other services. A secret exposed at a small unrelated service can therefore threaten a reused email password.'),
   p('Choose a separate, unpredictable secret for each account. A manager-generated password is one option; independently selected random words can help form a memorable passphrase. A birthday, song lyric or repeated seasonal pattern is easier to predict. Adding one symbol to a widely known password does not make it a new unpredictable secret. Every example printed in a lesson is public, so it is unsuitable as your actual password.'),
   p('For a centrally verified password used alone, current NIST guidance requires at least15characters; it allows a shorter minimum when that password is only part of MFA. This is guidance for service implementers, not proof that every site follows the rule. For your own account, favour a long unique generated secret that the service accepts. Length alone cannot repair a published or predictable value. Services also need protections such as checks against known weak secrets and throttling; those are not your browser’s responsibility alone.'),
   h('3. A password manager has a specific job'),
   term('Password manager and vault','A password manager stores account secrets in a protected vault and can generate distinct passwords. Its unlocking method, device security, recovery options and any sync account become important dependencies.'),
   p('Maya uses a manager so her email, learning account and code-hosting account do not share a password. She still protects the vault’s unlock secret and any recovery route. A manager is not permission to paste every secret into a school notebook. If autofill does not offer an expected credential, stop and check the site rather than forcing it; autofill behaviour can be a useful clue, but it is not an absolute website-safety test.'),
   p('Compare a trusted manager’s documentation before relying on it: how does the vault unlock, what happens if a device is lost, how are backups recovered, and how is its account protected? A cloud-synced vault adds access from other devices but depends on that service and account. An offline vault needs a separate recovery plan. Choose a model you can maintain rather than assuming either model eliminates every risk.'),
   h('4. Protect the account that resets other accounts'),
   term('Recovery dependency','A recovery email or other method can help regain an account. It also becomes a route that someone else might try to misuse. Protecting the primary login while neglecting recovery leaves another access path.'),
   p('Draw Maya’s fictional dependency chain: email helps reset the learning account; a manager supplies passwords; a phone helps authenticate. Losing email access may affect several services at once. Today, write only account roles, protection status and recovery questions. Do not put a password, recovery code, token or personal contact detail in Study Hub notes.'),
   h('5. Work through a decision'),
   p('Practice: Maya reused one password for email and a game account. The game service reports that account credentials may have been exposed. Explain why the email account is relevant even though its provider was not the reported source. A reasonable plan is to use a trusted route to change the affected/reused secrets to distinct ones, review access and prepare MFA/recovery. Routine calendar changes to already strong secrets are different from responding to evidence of compromise.'),
   key('Authentication establishes account control. Authorisation limits actions. Long unique secrets, a protected manager and protected recovery routes work together; no secret belongs in public lesson notes.'),
   tip('Your exercise is an account-role map, not a password collection. Use imaginary account names and mark unknown protection status as unknown.')
  ],
  checks:[
   check('Maya signs in successfully but cannot edit Ravi’s note. Which distinction explains this?','Authentication succeeded; authorisation does not allow the requested action on Ravi’s resource.','Account control and action permission are separate. Successful login is not universal access.'),
   check('A long password is a famous quotation. Is length enough to establish unpredictability?','No. A familiar quotation may be readily guessed.','Length is useful, but predictable selection or publication can defeat it. Do not reuse a lesson’s example as a secret.'),
   check('Why can an exposed game password matter to email?','If the email uses the same secret, someone may try the obtained pair there.','The risk follows reuse across services, not only the service that reported exposure.'),
   check('What should an account map contain instead of actual recovery codes?','Account roles, protected/not-checked status, recovery dependencies and next questions.','The learning exercise should describe protection without creating another copy of credentials.')
  ],
  reflection:'Create a fictional three-account map. For each account, state its purpose, the smallest needed access, whether its password is unique, and its recovery dependency. Explain which account you would prioritise and why.',
  references:[ref('NIST: password and authenticator guidance','https://pages.nist.gov/800-63-4/sp800-63b.html'),ref('NCSC: managing passwords','https://www.ncsc.gov.uk/collection/top-tips-for-staying-secure-online/password-managers')]
 },
 '4.2':{
  objective:'Explain authentication factors, distinguish OTPs from passkeys, follow a session and token lifecycle, and prepare a recovery/permission plan with clear limits.',
  sections:[
   h('1. Two steps are not automatically two factors'),
   term('Factor and multi-factor authentication (MFA)','A factor is a category of authentication evidence: something you know, have or are. MFA combines distinct factors. Two knowledge secrets, such as a password and a memorised answer, are not automatically two different factors.'),
   p('Maya enters a password and then uses an authenticator she controls. The goal is to reduce reliance on a password alone. Methods vary: a text message, an app code, a push approval or a cryptographic authenticator does not provide identical protection. Use a stronger supported method where practical, while keeping recovery usable. An unexpected approval request is not a task to accept just to make the notifications stop.'),
   term('One-time password (OTP) and approval prompt','An OTP is a limited-use code provided by an authenticator or service. A push prompt asks the user to approve an authentication event. Codes can be tricked out of a user, and prompts can be approved for an event the user did not start.'),
   p('Worked decision: Maya receives an approval prompt while she is only reading an offline lesson. She did not initiate a login. She does not approve it; through the known service route she checks account activity and available protection. The prompt alone is a reason to investigate, not proof of which person caused it. Never give a live code to someone claiming to be a support agent.'),
   h('2. A passkey uses a different authentication design'),
   term('Passkey and public/private key pair','A passkey uses a cryptographic credential tied to a service. The service stores a public key and checks a proof made using the private key. A device unlock such as a PIN or biometric may authorise use of the credential; that unlock is not the website’s shared password.'),
   term('Phishing-resistant authentication','Authentication designed so that a credential proof cannot simply be reused at an impersonating site. WebAuthn credentials are associated with the intended service, helping resist a fake lookalike login page.'),
   p('Some passkeys sync through a protected provider account; others remain on a specific device or security key. Learn where yours is available and how it can be recovered. Calling something a passkey does not by itself answer every factor or account-recovery question. Protection still depends on implementation, device access and fallback routes. Passkeys do not make a harmful attachment harmless or prevent every kind of session misuse.'),
   h('3. What happens after sign-in?'),
   term('Session, cookie and bearer token','A session is continuity of access after authentication. A cookie is browser-managed site data often used to carry a session identifier. A bearer token generally grants its permitted access to whoever presents it, subject to the service’s checks, expiry and revocation.'),
   p('Simplified journey: authenticate → service establishes session → browser presents a session credential on later requests → service checks the requested action. You do not normally type your password for every page. That convenience means the session credential also needs protection. The raw value is not appropriate for a screenshot or a public diagnostic note.'),
   term('Expiry, revocation and scope','Expiry is the time after which a credential should no longer be accepted. Revocation invalidates it before then through the provider. Scope describes permitted operations or resources; it should fit the task rather than grant everything.'),
   p('A fictional progress-sync token should access only the necessary progress resource under that provider’s permission model. A real provider may not offer the exact fine-grained scope you wish for, so verify its documented permissions. A token is not a harmless account label. Logging out of one browser does not prove every API token or other-device session was revoked. Changing a password may also leave some independent credentials active; use the service’s session/token management when appropriate.'),
   h('4. Recovery is another part of the system'),
   term('Recovery code and fallback method','A recovery code can help regain access when a normal authenticator is unavailable. A fallback is an alternative account-access route. Both require protection, and their supported behaviour depends on the provider.'),
   p('Practice: Maya plans for a lost phone before losing it. She checks official recovery instructions, protects supported backup methods and keeps recovery material outside public study notes. She can explain the recovery route without revealing the code. If her only copy is on the lost device, the plan may fail precisely when needed. If an insecure fallback bypasses strong authentication, it can weaken the overall account.'),
   h('5. Design a small access plan'),
   p('Write a fictional plan with five rows: preferred authentication method, device/provider dependency, recovery route, session-review method and minimal integration permission. Mark each row as documented or still unknown. You are practising reasoning about the full access lifecycle, not collecting real credentials or building a homemade login system.'),
   key('MFA, passkeys, sessions, tokens and recovery solve related but different problems. Protect each credential and limit its lifetime and access where the provider supports it.')
  ],
  checks:[
   check('Are a password and a memorised security answer automatically two different factors?','No. Both are knowledge-based evidence.','Count factor categories and implementation, not merely the number of screens.'),
   check('Maya did not start a login but receives an approval prompt. What should she do?','Do not approve it. Check through the known service route and review activity/protection.','An unsolicited prompt should not be accepted simply to stop repeated requests.'),
   check('Does logging out of one browser prove every API token is invalid?','No. Sessions and separate credentials can have different lifecycles.','Check the provider’s actual revocation, token and other-device session controls.'),
   check('What does a passkey still require planning for?','Device/provider access, recovery, fallback methods and session protection.','Resistance to lookalike login sites does not eliminate every account or endpoint risk.')
  ],
  reflection:'Draw a fictional login → session → request → logout journey. Label which evidence establishes identity, which secret maintains access, which rule limits actions, and how a lost authenticator would be handled.',
  references:[ref('MDN: WebAuthn and passkeys','https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Web_Authentication_API'),ref('MDN: cookies','https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Guides/Cookies'),ref('NIST: authenticator lifecycle and recovery','https://pages.nist.gov/800-63-4/sp800-63b.html')]
 },
 '4.3':{
  objective:'Recognise a request that needs independent verification, inspect a fictional URL’s host, and choose a calm phishing response without relying on appearance alone.',
  sections:[
   h('1. The target can be a decision, not a computer flaw'),
   term('Social engineering and phishing','Social engineering manipulates people into actions such as sharing information or granting access. Phishing uses deceptive messages or destinations to impersonate a trusted interaction. It can seek secrets, approvals, payments or software execution.'),
   p('Imagine Maya receives a polished message claiming her learning account will close in ten minutes. It asks her to enter a login code through a supplied link. The important observation is the requested action under pressure. A familiar logo, correct name and good spelling do not establish who sent it. A genuine account notice should still be verified through a separately known route.'),
   term('Pretext, urgency and authority','A pretext is the story used to justify a request. Urgency reduces time to think, and claimed authority pressures cooperation. These are indicators to examine rather than automatic proof that every urgent message is fraudulent.'),
   p('Do not build a rule saying bad grammar means phishing and good grammar means safe. Deceptive messages can be carefully written, and a legitimate message can contain errors. Likewise, an unexpected message is not enough to identify a particular attacker. Describe the request, what independent evidence you have and what remains unknown.'),
   h('2. Read the destination rather than the logo'),
   term('URL host, path and lookalike','The host identifies the destination name in a URL; the path identifies a resource there. A trusted-looking word inside the path or inside another host does not establish the intended service. These examples use reserved .example names and are text to inspect, not working login links.'),
   p('Fictional expected host: accounts.school.example. Compare:\nhttps://accounts.school.example/security\nhttps://accounts.school.example.attacker.example/security\nhttps://attacker.example/accounts.school.example\nIn the second URL the host is accounts.school.example.attacker.example, not the expected host. In the third, the expected-looking string is in the path. None of those words grants trust to the destination.'),
   p('Read the complete host, not just its first familiar-looking characters. URL structures have edge cases, so a beginner exercise is not a universal domain-validation tool. A shortened link or a QR code can also hide the destination before opening it. For an unexpected sensitive request, choose a known bookmark, previously verified official app or independently obtained contact route rather than guessing from the message.'),
   h('3. Decide without interacting with the lure'),
   p('Worked example: the message says Maya must supply an OTP to keep her course progress. She pauses, does not submit the code, and opens the known learning service independently. She checks whether the account actually has a notice. If help is needed, she uses the official contact route, not a number inside the suspicious message. Her goal is to verify the claimed task, not to investigate a suspicious site by logging into it.'),
   term('Independent verification','Confirming a claim through a trusted route that was not supplied by the suspicious request. A reply in the same conversation may just reach the same impersonator.'),
   p('A message from a known address can also come from an account whose access was misused. Familiar sender identity is context, not a guarantee. If a classmate unexpectedly requests a payment or a secret, confirm the request through another established channel. Your report should separate the sender displayed by the interface from the identity you actually verified.'),
   h('4. Respond according to what occurred'),
   p('Reading a message, opening a destination, submitting a password, approving a prompt and running a download are different events. Do not claim a complete account compromise merely because a message was read. If a credential was submitted, use a known service route from a trusted device to change/revoke the affected access and review activity. If software was run or the device may be affected, involve the responsible administrator or use the official recovery/support process. Week7 will expand incident response.'),
   p('For a fictional report, record the time, the requested action, the route used to verify it and what response was taken. Preserve appropriate evidence through the organisation’s permitted process. Do not forward live secret values or personal message contents into public course notes. Use the service or organisation’s established reporting channel.'),
   h('5. Practise choosing the next action'),
   p('ScenarioA: a bank-themed text demands an OTP. Next action: use the bank’s already known app or contact route, not the text’s link. ScenarioB: a classmate’s usual account requests a sudden transfer. Next action: independently confirm the task. ScenarioC: a security notice is present in the independently opened official account. Next action: follow the verified service workflow. Good reasoning depends on the evidence and task, not a blanket rule to trust or reject every message.'),
   key('Pause, identify the requested action, verify through an independent known route, and respond to what actually happened. Appearance and HTTPS alone do not establish a legitimate request.')
  ],
  checks:[
   check('A message has perfect spelling and your name. Does that establish authenticity?','No. Presentation and personal details can be copied or obtained.','Verify the task through a separately known route rather than using appearance as proof.'),
   check('Is accounts.school.example.attacker.example the same host as accounts.school.example?','No. The complete hostname differs.','A familiar string at the start of another name does not make it the intended host.'),
   check('You read a suspicious text but entered nothing and ran no download. Should you report that a password was definitely stolen?','No. Describe the events you actually observed.','Different interactions expose different things. An unsupported diagnosis makes a report less useful.'),
   check('An unexpected request comes from a known classmate’s account. What remains worth verifying?','Whether the classmate really initiated and intends the requested action.','A familiar account can be misused; independent confirmation checks the task, not only the displayed sender.')
  ],
  reflection:'Invent two short messages requesting different sensitive actions. For each, identify the pretext, uncertainty, destination evidence and independent verification route. Do not send the messages or use real names, credentials or working login pages.',
  references:[ref('FTC: recognising and responding to phishing','https://consumer.ftc.gov/articles/how-recognize-avoid-phishing-scams'),ref('MDN: URL structure','https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Learn_web_development/Howto/Web_mechanics/What_is_a_URL')]
 },
 '4.4':{
  objective:'Choose between encoding, encryption and hashing from the security goal, explain the role of keys and trusted baselines, and describe Python’s supporting role without implementing cryptography.',
  sections:[
   h('1. Three transformations, three different questions'),
   term('Encoding','Encoding changes representation so data can be stored or transmitted in a compatible form. It is not intended to keep the content secret. Base64 is one example and can be decoded without a secret key.'),
   term('Encryption','Encryption uses cryptographic keys to protect confidentiality. Approved decryption with the appropriate key recovers the original plaintext. Real systems also need integrity protection and correct handling of keys and parameters.'),
   term('Cryptographic hash and digest','A hash maps bytes to a digest under a defined algorithm. A secure cryptographic hash is designed to make recovering the input or finding certain different inputs with the same digest impractical. It is not reversible encryption and is not a guarantee of mathematical uniqueness.'),
   p('Start with the goal. If another system needs a compatible text representation, encoding may fit. If an authorised recipient must recover protected content, encryption may fit. If you want to compare a file with a trusted recorded fingerprint, a hash may fit. A transformed-looking string is not evidence that the goal was met.'),
   h('2. Worked choices using a fictional study file'),
   p('Maya’s fictional file contains a lesson schedule, not a password. CaseA: the transport format needs bytes represented as text. Base64 can encode them; for example, the text study in UTF-8 becomes c3R1ZHk= in Base64. Anyone with the representation can decode it. CaseB: a private archive must later be read by Maya. She needs a maintained encryption tool and a protected recovery/key plan. CaseC: she wants to detect an unexpected change to a file. She records a cryptographic digest and protects that baseline.'),
   term('Plaintext, ciphertext and key','Plaintext is the input information. Ciphertext is the encrypted form. A key is cryptographic material controlling the operation; placing the key next to publicly shipped ciphertext may defeat the intended confidentiality.'),
   p('Encryption protects against particular access paths. An encrypted drive can help protect stored data when the device is locked, but an application running after unlock may still read data available to that user. TLS protects a network connection; it does not turn a public lesson into private content. Different layers cover different boundaries.'),
   h('3. Why a digest is not a hidden copy of a password'),
   term('Guess comparison','An attacker can hash a guessed input and compare it with an obtained digest. A one-way hash does not make a low-entropy or common input impossible to discover by guessing.'),
   p('A password has human-selection patterns and often less unpredictability than a random file. Fast file-hash algorithms permit many guesses, so password storage needs a specialised design. Day5 distinguishes a file checksum from a password-verification record. Never conclude that replacing a secret in public JavaScript with its plain SHA-256 digest is a complete login design.'),
   p('Hashes also operate on exact bytes. A changed line ending or encoding can produce a different digest even when displayed words look similar. A digest mismatch tells you the compared bytes do not match that baseline; it does not alone tell you whether the cause was an attacker, an edit or a transfer problem. Document the algorithm, file identity and expected source.'),
   h('4. Protect the key and the baseline'),
   term('Key management and authenticated encryption','Key management concerns creation, storage, access, replacement and recovery of keys. Authenticated encryption is a design that protects confidentiality and detects unauthorised changes under its assumptions. Use established libraries/tools rather than inventing a classroom cipher for important data.'),
   p('A private file and the only decryption key stored on one lost device may be unrecoverable. An unlocked shared folder containing both the archive and its key may offer little protection against someone who can read both. Ask who can access each part, what is kept separately and how recovery works. A secure algorithm name cannot answer these operational questions by itself.'),
   h('5. Where Python can support investigation'),
   p('Within an approved fictional folder, Python can read files as bytes, compute SHA-256 digests with a standard library, compare them against an approved baseline, and create a changed/unchanged/unavailable report. It should identify unreadable or missing files rather than pretending they matched. It should not publish private file contents or secret material. You are learning the security purpose today; writing the script belongs in a later properly scoped exercise.'),
   p('Practice design: make a paper table with file identity, algorithm, trusted expected digest, comparison result and limitation. Write changed for different digests, unavailable when the file cannot be read, and baseline unknown when no trusted expected value exists. Do not convert all three into malware detected.'),
   key('Encoding changes representation. Encryption protects recoverable information using keys. Hashing supports comparisons under a trusted baseline. Choose the operation from the goal, not the appearance of its output.')
  ],
  checks:[
   check('Does Base64 require a secret key to decode?','No. It is an encoding, not confidentiality protection.','A readable decoder can reverse the representation. A less familiar appearance does not make a value secret.'),
   check('Maya must later read a private archive. Is a file hash a replacement for encryption?','No. A hash is a digest, not a recoverable encrypted archive.','The requirement is protected recovery of information, which needs an appropriate encryption/key design.'),
   check('A file digest changes after a newline conversion. Does that prove malware?','No. Exact bytes changed, but the cause requires context.','Hash comparisons support integrity observations, not a complete attribution or malware diagnosis.'),
   check('How should an integrity report represent an unreadable file?','As unavailable or an explicit read failure, not unchanged.','A failed observation cannot establish that the bytes match a trusted baseline.')
  ],
  reflection:'Choose an operation for three fictional tasks: transporting bytes as text, protecting a recoverable archive and comparing a downloaded file. For each, state its goal, necessary trusted information and one thing it does not establish.',
  references:[ref('Python: Base64 representation','https://docs.python.org/3/library/base64.html'),ref('Python: cryptographic file digests','https://docs.python.org/3/library/hashlib.html'),ref('OWASP: cryptographic storage and key management','https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html')]
 },
 '4.5':{
  objective:'Explain why file digests and password hashing differ, interpret a checksum comparison, and separate HTTPS connection protection from content or business trust.',
  sections:[
   h('1. A file checksum answers a limited question'),
   term('Checksum and trusted expected digest','Checksum is a broad term for a value used to check data. For adversarial file-integrity checks, choose an appropriate cryptographic hash such as SHA-256 and obtain the expected digest from a trusted source. Simple error-detection checksums and weakened algorithms do not offer the same properties.'),
   p('Worked example: an approved fictional publisher offers an installer and a SHA-256 digest through a verified route. Maya computes the digest of the downloaded file’s exact bytes using the same algorithm. A mismatch means it does not match the expected file. A match supports agreement with that trusted reference, under the hash’s assumptions. It does not prove the publisher’s program is harmless or suitable for her machine.'),
   term('Baseline authenticity','The expected value must come from a source whose authenticity you can justify. If an adversary replaces both a file and an unprotected accompanying hash, comparing those two can still produce a match.'),
   p('Think of the file and baseline as two evidence items. Checking against a digest from the same unverified message does not independently establish origin. Signed release information can provide a different kind of origin evidence when its signing identity is validated. Today the key lesson is the trust path, not installing unfamiliar verification software or bypassing a security warning.'),
   h('2. A password record has another purpose'),
   term('Password hashing, salt and work cost','Password hashing uses a specialised password-verification design. A salt is a distinct value stored with a record so equal passwords do not simply produce interchangeable records. A work cost increases the computation/resources required for guesses. A salt is not a replacement for password unpredictability.'),
   p('A service should not need to recover the plaintext password to verify a later login. Maintained implementations of password-hashing designs such as Argon2id can produce verification records that include needed parameters and salts. This is different from making a quick SHA-256 fingerprint of a file. Do not build your own password system by copying a generic digest into a database.'),
   p('Salt values are not normally treated as secret like passwords. Their purpose is not to encrypt the password. A stolen verifier record may still be subjected to guessing, so strong passwords and appropriate work costs matter. The application also needs protected transport, rate limiting, access control and secure recovery. A storage primitive alone is not an account system.'),
   h('3. TLS protects the connection'),
   term('TLS, HTTPS and certificate','TLS protects communication between endpoints, including confidentiality and integrity. HTTPS applies it to HTTP. A certificate participates in the browser’s check of the server’s identity for the requested name through a recognised trust process.'),
   p('A valid HTTPS connection helps the browser communicate with the named endpoint without undetected network modification under its security assumptions. It does not certify that every claim on the page is honest. An impersonating site can obtain HTTPS for its own lookalike hostname. You still need to confirm the intended destination and the requested task.'),
   p('Worked choice: a polished page on the wrong host has HTTPS. Maya does not enter a credential merely because the connection is encrypted. She independently opens the intended service. In another case, the correct service shows a certificate warning. She stops the sensitive action and checks the URL, device clock and official support status through a known route. She does not disable certificate validation just to complete the login.'),
   h('4. Follow the boundaries after delivery'),
   term('Data in transit and data at rest','In transit means moving between endpoints. At rest means stored on a device or service. HTTPS covers a connection; it does not guarantee encrypted local files, confidential page content or protection from code running at an endpoint.'),
   p('Your learning site can be delivered over HTTPS while its authored lesson text remains intentionally public. A browser can store notes locally, and a sync service can receive selected data when explicitly used. These are separate storage and access decisions. Do not infer that a public HTML file is private because the address begins with https. Day6 uses this distinction to plan backups and sensitive-data handling.'),
   h('5. Practise with three pieces of evidence'),
   p('For a fictional download, write three separate conclusions: whether the host is the intended source, whether the browser accepted the secure connection, and whether the file bytes match a trusted release digest. Mark any unknown item as unknown. Then explain why even three positive observations do not constitute a complete malware or software-quality review. The habit is to state exactly what your evidence supports.'),
   key('File hashing supports byte-integrity checks. Password hashing slows verification-record guessing. HTTPS protects a named connection; it does not guarantee honest content or private stored data.')
  ],
  checks:[
   check('A file and its expected digest came from the same unverified message. Does a match independently establish the publisher?','No. Both could have been supplied or replaced together.','The expected digest needs a trustworthy origin; agreement alone does not authenticate that origin.'),
   check('Why is plain fast SHA-256 an unsuitable complete password-storage design?','Passwords can be guessed; specialised password hashing adds appropriate salts and work costs.','A file digest and a login verification record have different threat models and operational requirements.'),
   check('A lookalike host uses valid HTTPS. Is its request therefore legitimate?','No. HTTPS can protect the connection to that wrong host.','Connection protection does not establish that the destination is the service you intended or that its request is honest.'),
   check('Does HTTPS automatically encrypt a downloaded file on your disk?','No. Stored-data protection is a separate layer.','A network connection and the resulting local file have different security boundaries.')
  ],
  reflection:'Write a fictional evidence table for a download: intended host, connection status, digest source, comparison and remaining questions. Separately explain why you would use a maintained password-verification system rather than a file checksum for an account.',
  references:[ref('Python: hash algorithm and digest limitations','https://docs.python.org/3/library/hashlib.html'),ref('OWASP: password storage','https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Password_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html'),ref('MDN: TLS and HTTPS boundaries','https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/Security/Defenses/Transport_Layer_Security')]
 },
 '4.6':{
  objective:'Design a recoverable and private backup plan, distinguish browser data from public website files, and describe a measured response to an exposed secret.',
  sections:[
   h('1. A copy is useful only if it can be recovered'),
   term('Backup, sync and version history','A backup preserves data for later recovery. Sync propagates changes between locations; it can also propagate deletion or damage. Version history may retain earlier states, but retention, access and recovery behaviour depend on the service.'),
   p('Maya syncs a fictional study file between her laptop and phone. That helps access the current version. It does not automatically prove she can recover yesterday’s version after an accidental deletion. A local copy on the same disk also shares that disk’s failure risk. Ask which failure the copy can survive and whether the right person can restore it.'),
   term('Retention, separation and recovery objective','Retention describes how long usable earlier copies remain. Separation reduces shared failure paths such as one damaged device or compromised account affecting every copy. Recovery objectives describe how much work you can afford to lose and how quickly you need useful access back.'),
   p('For a study plan, losing one day’s notes might be acceptable; losing a month may not be. Design a schedule accordingly rather than selecting a backup interval by habit. Consider a protected separate copy and the credentials/keys needed to read it. An offline copy can avoid some connected failure paths, but only if it is actually disconnected when appropriate, maintained and stored safely. The number of copies alone is not a recovery test.'),
   h('2. Practise restoration without overwriting real progress'),
   term('Restore test','A restore test checks that a retained copy can be read and produces the intended usable result in a separate controlled destination. Seeing a filename or an export-success message is not enough.'),
   p('Fictional exercise: Maya creates two invented notes, exports a sample backup, opens a separate test profile and imports there. She checks the notes, the expected dates and how merge versus replacement behaves. She keeps her real browser/profile untouched. Write down the backup date, data included, restore result and remaining dependencies. If the file is encrypted, confirm that recovery of its key is also possible.'),
   p('A backup can contain personal journal entries even when it contains no token. Keep it out of a public source repository and protect access to the copy. Encryption can help confidentiality but does not remove the need for recoverable keys or clear access rules. Avoid making the only readable copy dependent on the account or device you are trying to recover from.'),
   h('3. Public site files and browser notes are different stores'),
   term('Public source, browser storage and cloud sync','Public source files contain information shipped to visitors, such as lesson text and application code. Browser storage holds data in a local browser context. Cloud sync sends data according to the application’s configured behaviour. These locations have different readers and access rules.'),
   p('Anyone allowed to download a website’s public HTML or JavaScript can inspect its contents. Moving a credential from HTML to a public JS file, Base64 text or another downloaded asset does not make it secret. A private database behind a properly controlled backend is a different architecture, but putting a database file into a public website is still public delivery. This lesson does not change Study Hub into a server application.'),
   term('localStorage is not a credential vault','localStorage is readable by JavaScript running in its origin context and persists in that browser until removed. It is not shared publicly merely because the public code refers to a storage key, but it should not be treated as a secure vault against hostile same-origin scripts or access to the browser profile.'),
   p('Opening a public course page on someone else’s phone does not automatically display your personal local notes from your phone. Explicit sharing, sync, a shared profile or compromised access can change the situation. Protect the browser/device and understand which data a configured sync route uploads. A hidden text field or obscured UI is a presentation choice, not a robust access boundary. Keep actual credentials and recovery codes outside lesson notes and ordinary backup content.'),
   h('4. An exposed secret needs an access response'),
   term('Revocation, rotation and exposure','Exposure means secret material may have become accessible outside its intended boundary. Revocation invalidates access associated with a credential; rotation replaces a secret under a controlled process. Removing the latest visible text does not invalidate copies or older repository history.'),
   p('Worked incident: a fictional token was accidentally committed to a public repository. First use the provider’s known trusted route to revoke or rotate the exposed credential, then review its allowed actions and relevant activity. Remove the exposure using the repository/provider’s supported process, including historical copies where necessary. Never test the leaked credential by using it in a classroom script. A source cleanup is not proof that every previous reader forgot the value.'),
   p('Record only the credential’s role, the discovery time, the affected access and response status. Do not copy the value into tickets, notes or a screenshot. Repository-history rewriting can affect collaborators and should be planned separately; this lesson explains the order of concerns without changing your repository history or account settings.'),
   h('5. Where Python can assist without becoming an access bypass'),
   p('For approved sample data, Python can check whether required backup files exist, compare integrity digests, summarise restore-test results, or flag patterns that might indicate accidental secret inclusion. Such a report should show a file/field location and category with the value redacted, not print candidate secrets. Pattern matching can miss secrets and can flag innocent text; it is a triage aid, not proof that a repository is clean or a credential is real.'),
   h('6. Build a practical protection plan'),
   p('Create a paper plan for a fictional study archive: what is backed up, where separate copies live, who can read them, retention, how recovery material is protected, and the latest successful test. Add a publication rule: authored lessons may be public; personal backups, tokens and journals require their own private handling. Choose a next test you can perform with invented data rather than putting real secrets into an exercise.'),
   key('Backup includes recoverability and privacy. Public code, local notes and configured sync are separate stores. Revoke exposed access before assuming a source-file cleanup solved it.')
  ],
  checks:[
   check('A sync service copies a deletion to the second device. Was the second device automatically an independent recovery copy?','No. Sync can propagate the same loss.','You need to know retained versions, separation and restore behaviour, not merely count devices.'),
   check('Where should a first import/restore exercise run?','In a separate controlled test destination/profile with fictional data.','The test should demonstrate recovery without risking live progress through replacement.'),
   check('Does moving a token into a public JavaScript file hide it from visitors?','No. Downloaded public code can be inspected.','File type, encoding and hidden UI do not change the public delivery boundary.'),
   check('A public token was deleted from the newest file. What access action remains necessary?','Revoke or rotate it through the provider and review relevant activity/permissions.','Older history or copies may still contain a usable value. Deleting text does not invalidate the credential.')
  ],
  reflection:'Design a fictional backup-and-publication plan, including one restore test and an exposure-response sequence. In a second paragraph, explain what Python may flag, what it must redact and why its scan cannot prove the absence of secrets.',
  references:[ref('NCSC: backing up important data','https://www.ncsc.gov.uk/collection/top-tips-for-staying-secure-online/always-back-up-your-most-important-data'),ref('OWASP: browser storage boundaries','https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/HTML5_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html'),ref('GitHub: responding to sensitive repository data','https://docs.github.com/en/authentication/keeping-your-account-and-data-secure/removing-sensitive-data-from-a-repository')]
 },
 '4.7':{
  objective:'Connect account protection, phishing verification, cryptographic boundaries and recovery in one fictional case; explain each decision and uncertainty before checking the model answers.',
  sections:[
   h('1. Retrieve the ideas before reading the answers'),
   p('Use this day for active recall and rest, not another tool installation. Close your earlier notes for the first attempt. Define authentication, authorisation, MFA, session, scope, encoding, encryption, digest, trusted baseline and restore test in your own words. Then reconnect each term to a concrete action. If you remember a name but cannot explain the boundary it covers, review that specific day rather than rereading the whole week without a purpose.'),
   term('Active recall and decision evidence','Active recall means attempting to retrieve an idea before looking it up. Decision evidence explains which observation supports an action, what remains unknown and which access boundary is involved. A correct label without a reason is incomplete practice.'),
   h('2. One fictional case, several different risks'),
   p('Case file: Maya maintains an offline learning site. Its authored lessons are publicly hosted; her journal is stored in her browser. She uses a primary email, a password manager and a fictional sync integration. An urgent message claims her progress will disappear unless she gives a live login code. Later she notices a made-up token value in an old public commit. She also has a backup export but has never restored it. A download page displays HTTPS and an unfamiliar digest source.'),
   p('First separate the issues. The message asks for an authentication secret and needs independent task verification. The public commit concerns credential exposure and revocation. The untested export concerns recoverability. The download concerns destination, connection and baseline trust. The public lessons concern publication of intentionally shared content. These are not interchangeable proof that every store was read or that the whole device is compromised.'),
   h('3. Build an action sequence with reasons'),
   p('Before revealing answers, write an action sequence for the case. A model sequence is: do not supply the code; independently open the known service to check the claimed task; use the provider’s known route to revoke/rotate exposed integration access and review activity; plan supported source/history cleanup; validate a fictional restore in a separate destination; evaluate the intended download host and trusted digest source. The exact order of broader recovery can depend on whether the device or primary email is affected. State that dependency rather than pretending one checklist fits every incident.'),
   p('Now name the evidence for each step. An unexpected OTP request does not authorise sharing it. An old public commit can retain a value after a new commit removes it. A file labelled backup does not prove a successful restore. HTTPS concerns a named connection and does not certify the page’s claims. A digest needs a trusted expected value. Each reason supports a bounded conclusion.'),
   h('4. Apply the week to a shared-device decision'),
   p('Second exercise: Maya and Ravi share a lab device but use separate accounts. Ravi needs to read a worksheet, not manage Maya’s integrations. Choose minimal resource permissions; protect session access and avoid placing recovery material in the shared worksheet. When Maya signs out of the learning browser, do not automatically mark her independent integration token revoked. Refer to the provider’s actual credential controls. This brings Week3’s least-privilege model into Week4’s online-account lifecycle.'),
   h('5. Finish with a measurable weekly deliverable'),
   p('Your deliverable has four fictional documents: an account dependency map, an independently verified-message decision, a protection-choice table and a backup/restore plan. Each should state the task, the evidence, its limitation and the next action. For Python’s role, add one sentence about integrity reporting and one about redacted sample-data triage; do not turn them into password collection or scanning other people’s systems.'),
   p('Use the twelve checks below as an untimed closed-notes exercise. Write an answer and why before pressing Show answer. Revisit Day1 for identity/secret selection, Day2 for account lifecycle, Day3 for deceptive requests, Day4 for transformations, Day5 for integrity/HTTPS, and Day6 for recovery/publication. You have learned the foundation when you can choose the next action and explain what it does not prove.'),
   key('Protection is a chain of identity, permitted actions, trusted communication, careful storage and tested recovery. Match each conclusion to evidence; keep secrets out of the learning record.'),
   tip('Record difficult concepts and fictional reasoning in notes. Take a break after retrieval; tomorrow’s web-security topics will reuse these boundaries.')
  ],
  checks:[
   check('A valid login can read its own record but not another learner’s. Explain why.','Authentication establishes account control; authorisation limits resources/actions.','Access is decided for a particular operation, not granted universally by sign-in.'),
   check('What problem does a distinct password for every service reduce?','One service’s exposed credential being usable across reused accounts.','This is the cross-service reuse problem. A unique password still needs secrecy and account protection.'),
   check('Two memorised secrets appear on two screens. Is that sufficient evidence of two distinct factors?','No. Both can be knowledge evidence.','Factor categories and implementation matter, not the screen count.'),
   check('A passkey resists fake login hosts. Name two other protection dependencies.','Device/provider access and protected recovery/session handling.','Service-bound authentication does not eliminate endpoint, fallback or session risks.'),
   check('What should Maya do with an OTP requested by an unexpected urgent message?','Do not share it; independently verify the task through a known service route.','The request’s urgency does not establish its legitimacy.'),
   check('The familiar school name appears in a URL path on another host. What does it prove?','Only that the string is present in the path, not that the host is the school service.','Destination identity depends on the complete host and a trusted route, not a familiar substring.'),
   check('Choose encoding, encryption or hashing for compatible transport text, a recoverable private archive and a file comparison.','Encoding for representation; encryption for protected recovery; hashing for a digest comparison against a trusted baseline.','These operations serve different goals and have different trust/key requirements.'),
   check('A SHA-256 digest matches an expected value from an unverified message. Does that authenticate the publisher?','No. The trust of the expected value is not established.','Matching two supplied values cannot independently establish who supplied them or whether the software is safe.'),
   check('Why does password storage need a different design from a fast file fingerprint?','Password guessing needs appropriately costly verification records with salts and maintained implementations.','A generic digest alone does not address the account’s password threat model or lifecycle.'),
   check('Does valid HTTPS on a page establish honest content and encrypted local backups?','No. It protects a named connection, not every content or storage decision.','Connection, destination intent and stored-data protection are separate boundaries.'),
   check('What demonstrates that a backup is useful beyond the file existing?','A successful controlled restore with checks of expected usable data and recovery dependencies.','Test separately with fictional data so the exercise does not overwrite live progress.'),
   check('A token is removed from the newest public source but remains in history. What response is required?','Revoke/rotate the affected credential, review access/activity and plan supported source/history cleanup.','A source edit does not invalidate previously obtained copies; access response and cleanup are distinct.')
  ],
  reflection:'Complete the case action sequence and the four fictional deliverables. Identify your two least clear concepts, the specific day to revisit and one question for each. Leave all real passwords, token values, recovery codes and personal backup content out of notes.',
  references:[ref('NIST: account and authenticator guidance','https://pages.nist.gov/800-63-4/sp800-63b.html'),ref('FTC: independently verifying suspicious requests','https://consumer.ftc.gov/articles/how-recognize-avoid-phishing-scams'),ref('GitHub: sensitive-data response','https://docs.github.com/en/authentication/keeping-your-account-and-data-secure/removing-sensitive-data-from-a-repository')]
 }
};
